    14	
    15	# Item
    16	
    17	Defined in `backend/app/models.py` as `class Item(ItemBase, table=True)`.
    18	
    19	# Schema
    20	
    21	| Column | Type | Notes |
    22	|---|---|---|
    23	| `id` | UUID | Primary key |
    24	| `title` | string, 1–255 chars | Required |
    25	| `description` | string \| null, ≤255 | Optional |
    26	| `created_at` | datetime (tz-aware) | Set at insert time |
    27	| `owner_id` | UUID | FK → `user.id`, `nullable=False`, `ondelete="CASCADE"` |
    28	
    29	# Relationships
    30	
    31	- `owner: User | None` — back-populates `User.items`.
    32	
    33	# API-facing variants
    34	
    35	- `ItemCreate` — just `ItemBase` (title + description); `owner_id` is set server-side
    36	  from the authenticated user, never client-supplied (see `routers/items.md`).
    37	- `ItemUpdate` — both fields optional (partial update).
    38	- `ItemPublic` — adds `id`, `owner_id`, `created_at` back.
    39	
    40	# Authorization rule (enforced in `routers/items.py`, not at the DB layer)
    41	
    42	A non-superuser can only read/update/delete items where `item.owner_id == current_user.id`.
    43	Superusers can act on any item. This check is repeated in each of
    44	`read_item`/`update_item`/`delete_item` — there is no row-level security at the
    45	Postgres level, it's application-enforced.
    46	
    47	See [routers/items](../routers/items.md) for the endpoints.
